::: {.doc-subject}

Compute Adamic-Adar index for node pairs.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L36653-L36798){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.adamic_adar_index(ebunch=None)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Adamic-Adar index](../assets/graphs/adamic-adar-index.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Nodes B and E are outlined in the accent color with their shared neighbors C and D filled in accent; badges give C degree 3 and D degree 2. The card sums 1 over the log of each degree, 0.910 + 1.443, for an Adamic-Adar index of 2.353." width="100%"}

For each pair of nodes `(u, v)`, computes `Σ(1 / log(|N(w)|))` for all common neighbors `w`.
This gives more weight to common neighbors that themselves have fewer connections (rarer
connections are more significant). The logarithm is the natural log, so a shared
neighbor of degree 2 contributes about `1.44`, one of degree 3 about `0.91`, and a hub of
degree 100 only about `0.22`. The graph is not modified.

Adamic-Adar is one of the most reliable local link-prediction scores and is useful for:

- recommending connections where sharing a niche contact means more than sharing a
  celebrity contact
- ranking candidate links in social, collaboration, or biological networks
- comparing against `common_neighbors()` (no degree penalty) and
  `resource_allocation_index()` (a stronger penalty)

The measure is intended for undirected graphs. On a directed graph, neighbors and
degrees are taken from *outgoing* edges, and a shared neighbor with out-degree `0` or `1`
contributes nothing (its logarithm would be zero or undefined). When `ebunch=` is `None`,
every pair of non-adjacent nodes is scored, which is quadratic in the number of nodes;
pass `ebunch=` on large graphs.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[ebunch]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list[tuple[Union[int, str], Union[int, str]]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   List of `(u, v)` node pairs to score. Each node can be given as a node ID (`int`) or
    label (`str`). Pairs are scored even if `u` and `v` are already connected, and they
    are returned in the order given. If `None` (the default), all pairs of nodes that are
    not already connected (in either direction) are scored, each unordered pair once,
    smaller node ID first, in node ID order. A node that doesn't exist raises a `ValueError`.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [list[tuple[int, int, float]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   List of `(u, v, index)` tuples where index is the Adamic-Adar score (`0.0` when the
    nodes share no neighbors). Nodes are reported as node IDs.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If a node in `ebunch=` does not exist in the graph.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

The Adamic-Adar index refines common-neighbor counting by weighting each shared
neighbor $w$ by $\dfrac{1}{\log |N(w)|}$:
$\mathrm{AA}(u, v) = \sum_{w \in N(u) \cap N(v)} \dfrac{1}{\log |N(w)|}$. The
intuition, borrowed from information theory, is that rare neighbors carry more
evidence of a real connection than popular hubs everyone touches. The score is
unbounded above and empirically beats plain common-neighbor counting on many real
networks. Runs in $O(k)$ per pair.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~resource_allocation_index`) 

:   A sum of one over each shared neighbor's degree, not its log.

[](`~common_neighbors`) 

:   The number of shared neighbors.

[](`~jaccard_coefficient`) 

:   Shared neighbors over the two neighborhoods together.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Each common neighbor counts one over the log of its degree. `A` and `D`'s two common
neighbors have two neighbors each, so their score is $2 / \ln 2$:

::: {#4dcf1e20 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("A", "C"), ("B", "D"), ("C", "D"), ("D", "E")]
)
label = graph.get_label_from_id
scores = graph.adamic_adar_index([("A", "D"), ("B", "E")])

[(label(u), label(v), round(s, 3)) for u, v, s in scores]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
[('A', 'D', 2.885), ('B', 'E', 0.91)]
```
:::
:::


Each node labeled with its number of neighbors. `B` and `C` each count `1 / ln 2` toward
the score of `A` and `D`. Toward `B` and `E`, `D` counts only `1 / ln 3`, since it has
three neighbors:

::: {#3339a7d2 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
spots = {"A": (0, 1), "B": (1, 2), "C": (1, 0), "D": (2, 1), "E": (3, 1)}
graph.set_node_attrs(
    "pos", {graph.get_id_from_label(v): p for v, p in spots.items()}
)
tag = {
    n: f"{label(n)} ({graph.get_degree(n)})" for n in graph.get_node_ids()
}

graph.set_node_attrs("tag", tag).plot(
    layout="pos", highlight={"accent": {"A", "D"}, "soft": {"B", "C"}},
    label_by="tag", height=240,
)
```

